    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d338">

                <pb facs="0674" n="642" xml:id="pg642"/>

                <head>338. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Mission at the United Nations<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d338-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1957-06-13T20:47:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 13,
                                1957—8:47 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>947. For Ambassador Lodge. I appreciate
                    thoughts in your telegram 1028<anchor n="2" xml:id="d338-f2"/> on desirability
                    taking advantage of present relatively improved situation to make new moves
                    toward settlement basic Palestine problems. We have been giving careful
                    consideration to what steps might be taken and share your view that forthcoming
                    General Assembly may provide opportunity for substantial efforts. We are not
                    convinced, however, that at this stage we can encourage or join in any
                    multi-nation approach to Palestine problems. Rather, we feel that an attempt to
                    obtain some movement on individual segments of Palestine problem offers most
                    immediate favorable prospects. Thus we are giving careful study to refugee
                    problem. Following formulation of US approach to this problem, we may be in
                    better position to assess chances of moving ahead with other nations prior to
                    and in GA.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
